BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-index", "after": "section-1-p2", "title": "The index as a twisted partition function", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A supersymmetric theory has a conserved fermion number $(-1)^F$ and supercharges that pair every state of non-zero energy with a partner of opposite statistics. In the trace $\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-\beta H}(-1)^F$ such pairs cancel, so only states annihilated by some supercharges, the BPS states, contribute, and the result does not change with $\beta$ or with any continuous coupling. For strings on $\mathbb R^d$ the relevant object is a half-BPS index that counts states with given momentum and winding charges; it can be computed at zero string coupling and gives $S_\text{micro}=4\pi\sqrt{|Q_nQ_w|}$. The paper's observation is that this index is also a point in a continuous family: since rotating by $2\pi$ gives $(-1)^F=e^{2\pi iJ}$, inserting $e^{-\beta\Omega J}$ with $\beta\Omega=2\pi i$ into the thermal partition function produces exactly the index, so one can turn the angular velocity on gradually and watch each saddle of the thermal problem move towards $\nu=0$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-thermal-circle", "after": "section-1-p3", "title": "The thermal circle and the winding tachyon", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A thermal partition function $\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-\beta H}$ equals a path integral in imaginary (Euclidean) time, with time periodic of length $\beta=1/T$: the trace glues the final state to the initial one. A thermal system therefore lives on space times a circle. A particle can only move around this circle, but a closed string can also wrap it, and the wrapped string's mass squared is its stretching energy, which grows with the circle, minus a zero-point energy. On a small enough circle, at high enough temperature, the zero-point term wins and the wrapped string becomes tachyonic. A tachyon is a direction in which the Euclidean action is unbounded below, so the partition function diverges: this is the Euclidean face of the Hagedorn growth of string states. Just before that point the wrapped string is a light field, with mass squared proportional to $R-R_H$, and that light field is what the Atick–Witten theory keeps.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-melvin", "after": "section-1-p4", "title": "How a twist becomes a magnetic field", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Take flat space with periodic Euclidean time, $x^0\sim x^0+2\pi$, and identify points so that going once around the time circle also rotates the $x^1x^2$ plane by an angle $2\pi\nu$. Locally nothing changes: the space is flat. Now reduce on the time circle, writing the metric as $e^\sigma(dx^0+a_idx^i)^2$ plus a spatial part. In the angle $\theta=\theta'-\nu x^0$, for which the identification is untwisted, the metric acquires a cross term $\nu\rho^2\,dx^0d\theta$. The Kaluza–Klein vector $a$ therefore has a profile whose field strength is concentrated near the axis $\rho=0$: a magnetic field, together with a circle radius and a dilaton that vary with $\rho$. This is a Euclidean Melvin flux tube. Any state with momentum or winding around the time circle is charged under these vectors and feels the field, which is how the paper's W boson sees the twist.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-enhancement", "after": "subsec-worldsheet-p5", "title": "Gauge enhancement at the self-dual radius", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>T-duality maps a closed string on a circle of radius $R$ to one on a circle of radius $1/R$ (string units), exchanging its momentum $n$ and winding $w$; $R=1$ is the fixed point, the self-dual radius. The string's left- and right-moving momenta are $p_{L,R}\propto n/R\pm wR$, and each side contributes separately to its mass. At a generic radius only $n=w=0$ states are massless, and the circle supplies two abelian gauge fields, the Kaluza–Klein vector and the $B$-field vector, or in a chiral basis $U(1)_L\times U(1)_R$. At the self-dual radius the states with $n=w=\pm1$ have $p_R=0$, while their $p_L$ exactly cancels the zero-point energy of the heterotic string's bosonic side. They become two extra massless vectors of charge $\pm1$ under $U(1)_L$, and the gauge group is enhanced to $SU(2)_L$: the textbook example of stringy gauge enhancement. The radius is then a flat direction of an adjoint scalar, and moving along it Higgses $SU(2)_L$ back to $U(1)_L$. That is why, near the index, the thermal winding string can be a gauge boson.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-landau", "after": "subsec-mag_spectrum-p1", "title": "Landau levels and the Zeeman shift", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A charged particle moving in a plane with a constant magnetic field $B$ has quantised circular orbits. For a relativistic particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ the energies are $E^2=m^2+qB(2k+1)$, with $k=0,1,2,\dots$: the Landau levels. Each level is hugely degenerate, with one state per flux quantum, and the lowest is a Gaussian of width $1/\sqrt{qB}$. A particle with spin adds a Zeeman term: the spin component $s$ along the field shifts the energy squared by $2qBs$. For a scalar the lowest level, $m^2+qB$, is always positive. For a spin-1 boson with $s=-1$ it is $m^2-qB$, which turns negative above a critical field: the Ambjørn–Olesen instability. The Melvin field of the paper is not uniform, and that lifts the degeneracy, leaving one lowest state to condense.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-saddles", "after": "sec-w_stars-p2", "title": "Saddles, their weight, and decoupling", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In the Euclidean path integral of gravity, a partition function is approximated by its classical solutions with the right boundary conditions, the saddles. Each contributes roughly $e^{-I}$, with $I$ its on-shell action, so the saddle of least action dominates; free energy, entropy and energy follow from derivatives of $I$ with respect to $\beta$. A Euclidean black hole is such a saddle, and so is a string star. Each is trustworthy only where its own approximation holds: weak curvature for the black hole, a light winding mode for the string star. When a parameter is tuned so that a saddle's trustworthy regime runs off to infinite size or action, the saddle stops contributing: its weight goes to zero, or it can no longer be trusted anywhere. The paper calls this decoupling, and asks which saddles survive the approach to the index.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-correspondence", "after": "subsec-bh_w_trans-p1", "title": "Why a black hole might become a string", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>As a black hole evaporates its temperature rises and its horizon shrinks, until the horizon reaches the string length $l_s$. There the curvature is stringy and general relativity can no longer be trusted. Susskind, and later Horowitz and Polchinski, noticed that at this point the entropy of the black hole and the entropy of a single highly excited string of the same mass agree, up to factors of order one. This “correspondence principle” suggests that the black hole might not end abruptly but become a long string, perhaps one bound by its own gravity. The argument is only an estimate: neither description is reliable exactly at the matching point, so it cannot say whether the change is smooth. The paper makes the same kind of rough comparison near the index, between the W star and rotating black holes.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-rotating", "after": "subsec-bh_w_trans-p2", "title": "Rotating black holes at imaginary angular velocity", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A rotating black hole has a temperature $T=1/\beta$ and a horizon angular velocity $\Omega$, and in the grand canonical ensemble it is weighted by $e^{-\beta(H-\Omega J)}$. Its Euclidean continuation, needed for the path integral, is a real geometry only if the rotation parameter is continued too, $a=ib$, which makes $\Omega$ imaginary. Smoothness at the horizon then requires that going once around Euclidean time also rotates the azimuthal angle by the real angle $-i\beta\Omega=2\pi(1-\nu)$. Up to a full turn, which acts as $(-1)^F$, this is the twisted identification of the paper's partition function. A non-rotating black hole sits at $\nu=1$. Reaching the index, $\nu=0$, needs a full turn of $2\pi$, an extreme value: in that limit the Euclidean horizon grows without bound, which is the decoupling the paper finds.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-two-charge", "after": "subsec-charged_setup-p1", "title": "Two-charge BPS states and the two-charge black hole", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>A heterotic string wound $Q_w$ times around a circle and carrying $Q_n$ units of momentum along it can be BPS when only its left-moving oscillators are excited; the number of such states grows as $e^{4\pi\sqrt{|Q_nQ_w|}}$ (Dabholkar and Harvey), and this count is what the index gives. On the gravity side the same charges are carried by a two-charge black hole whose extremal limit has vanishing horizon area in supergravity, the small black hole. Whether higher-derivative ($\alpha'$) corrections give it a finite area equal to the microscopic entropy has been argued since the 1990s, and the recent proposals of a BPS saddle for the gravitational index belong to the same debate.</p>"""},
]
